\fbox{\parbox{0.94\columnwidth}{%
\textbf{Instruction:} \emph{``What is the average height for a male?''}\\[4pt]
\textbf{Formal:} ``Yes, men are taller than women on average. The worldwide average height for men is 175\,cm (69 inches, or 5 feet 9 inches)\ldots''\\[2pt]
\textbf{Casual:} ``Sure, men are generally taller than women. On average, guys around the world are about 175\,cm tall---roughly 5 feet 9 inches\ldots''\\[4pt]
\hfill NLI = 0.998 \emph{(content preserved)}\\[4pt]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
\toprule
GPT-4o & Likert & Pairwise \\
\midrule
Formal & 5 & \textbf{wins (both orders)} \\
Casual & \textbf{8} & loses \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}}
\caption{The Likert Blindspot in action. GPT-4o rates the casual variant higher (8 vs.\ 5) in absolute scoring, yet consistently prefers the formal variant in pairwise comparison---same judge, same texts, opposite conclusions. Across $n = 88$ such pairs, this pattern averages to Likert ATE $\approx 0$ (n.s.) while pairwise BT detects significant formal preference (63\%, $p < 0.001$). Content equivalence verified by NLI (0.998).}
\label{fig:blindspot_example}
\end{figure}

LLM judges are central infrastructure for NLP evaluation and alignment training (RLHF, DPO), where judge preferences serve as reward signals; any systematic bias propagates through both evaluation leaderboards and model development pipelines.
Style features alone explain most of the variance in judge scores \citep{murugadoss2025sos}: 76--92\% of observed preferences originate from surface-level style cues \citep{li2026preference}, with bias scores of 0.76--0.92 across five judges \citep{soumik2026judging}.
Despite this consensus, existing approaches each address individual requirements---multi-dimensional style analysis \citep{casse2026concise,wang2026fairjudge}, interventional design \citep{reber2025rate}, population-level correction \citep{dubois2025lc}---but no single framework integrates per-axis decomposition, interventional identification, per-instance diagnostics, and black-box applicability.
A further concern: standard Likert scoring produces ${\sim}60\%$ uninformative ties, and even non-tied pairs carry weaker signal than pairwise comparison---a limitation predicted by measurement theory \citep{thurstone1927law} but previously unquantified for LLM judges (Figure~\ref{fig:blindspot_example}).

We propose Controlled Style Decomposition (CSD), combining NLI-gated content control, per-axis style decomposition, and position-controlled pairwise comparison.
Inspired by interventional text analysis \citep{veitch2020adapting}, CSD operates in three stages: style-controlled rewriting along a single axis, NLI entailment verification, and bias measurement via forced-choice comparison.
CSD requires no access to judge internals and applies post-hoc to any black-box judge.
Our experiments reveal the \emph{Likert Blindspot} across four target judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, Llama-3.3-70B, Gemini-2.5-Flash), one negative control (Claude Sonnet 4), three benchmarks, and two primary style axes, with exploratory register evidence.
On formality data, Likert yields non-significant ATE ($p = 0.332$, $0.908$), while BT detects 63.0\% (GPT-4o, $p = 0.0004$) and 66.5\% (Qwen3-32B, $p < 0.0001$) formal preferences; verbosity shows 77.3\% BT ($p < 0.0001$) invisible to Likert ($p = 0.179$; Figure~\ref{fig:forest_plot}).

Our contributions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{A black-box controlled framework} for per-axis style bias detection via style-controlled rewriting and position-controlled pairwise comparison (\S\ref{sec:method}).
    \item \textbf{The Likert Blindspot:} Likert scoring systematically misses style biases that pairwise comparison detects---complete at moderate bias ($d \to 0$ at $n = 355$), ${>}60\%$ attenuation at stronger effects---across six judge$\times$axis configurations ($p < 0.001$ pairwise vs.\ $p > 0.17$ Likert), confirmed by seven statistical methods (Tables~\ref{tab:main_results}--\ref{tab:sensitivity}).
    \item \textbf{Per-axis confound structure:} GEE decomposition shows verbosity is style-dominated (Style OR $8$--$13$, Orig OR ${<}1$), formality conditional, register judge-dependent---determining which estimates require conditional analysis (Table~\ref{tab:gee}).
    \item \textbf{Practical implications:} audits require pairwise comparison \emph{and} per-axis decomposition; Likert-based findings may systematically underestimate bias (\S\ref{sec:conclusion}).
\end{itemize}

